 prisoners turned crew. The standoff in the cave, by torchlight, with an emerald skull between them. 'Share?' the Spanish governor suggested. Captain Jack Thorne laughed. Pirates share. Up to a point.\n\nThe island rose from the mist like a fist. an emerald skull led them past the reef — the reef that had the bones of a dozen ships in its teeth — to a beach of black sand. 'Eats ships,' the gunner muttered. 'Charming.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "It ended as these things do: the Spanish governor overreached, Captain Jack Thorne didn't, and the sea took the Spanish governor with the tide. Captain Jack Thorne stood in the surf, breathing hard, an emerald skull safe. 'Bury them decent,' Captain Jack Thorne ordered. Pirates have rules.\n\nThey made the ship with the tide and the devils both rising. the Spanish governor made it too — of course; villains are buoyant. The final fight was on the beach, in the surf, with an emerald skull in Captain Jack Thorne's coat and everything to lose.\n\nThey sailed for the Spanish Main with the gold and an emerald skull and the logbook's secrets. The crew's shares would make them rich. Captain Jack Thorne's share would make them legend. the gunner was already spending theirs on a tavern."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Port and Pardon', 'text': "Captain Jack Thorne took the logbook to the governor — not the Spanish governor's governor, the new one — and traded certain pages for a pardon, signed and sealed. The crew cheered. Respectability, it turns out, is its own adventure.\n\nSome of the crew shipped out again, chasing the logbook's caches. Captain Jack Thorne stayed — for a season. The sea calls, and Captain Jack Thorne always answers. But home, Captain Jack Thorne was learning, is also an answer.\n\nthe Spanish Main welcomed them with bells and rum. The gold was divided fair — the articles are the articles. an emerald skull's copies went to every crew member who'd earned one. The original stayed with Captain Jack Thorne."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Tide Returns', 'text': "freedom has a price. Full sail. The horizon, as always, was just the beginning.\n\nThe new crew was young and wide-eyed. Captain Jack Thorne read them the articles, including the added one. They cheered, the way crews do. Pirates are sentimentalists. It's why they survive.\n\nA year later, a new an emerald skull surfaced — they always do — and Captain Jack Thorne was packed before the ink dried. the gunner closed the tavern for 'renovations' and came along, because that's what friends do."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

